## Accounts

**Pick one of these two blocks and delete the other.**

### Two accounts

| Role | Account | Browser | MCP |
|---|---|---|---|
| A, attacker | `<email>` | Chrome | `browser-mcp` |
| B, victim | `<email>` | Firefox | `claudecodebrowser` |

Both mailboxes are open in their own browser for verification mail, invites and reset links. Chrome is primary; go to Firefox only for Account B's identifiers and to confirm an effect landed.

### One account

| Role | Account | Browser | MCP |
|---|---|---|---|
| A, only account | `<email>` | Chrome | `browser-mcp` |

There is no second account this time. Do not mark the cross-account columns of the matrix as not-applicable. Work the single-account ladder in the `coverage-matrix` skill in order: try to create a second account, then find the second principal that already exists inside this one (roles, scoped API tokens, share links, invites, integrations, the pre-login state), then use the unauthenticated replay, which proves broken authorization with no second account and no exposure to anyone. Only after all three does a cell become `blocked: no second account`.

`<If a second account can be created and I have not said otherwise, create one and tell me.>`
`<If a second mailbox exists but is not logged in anywhere, say so and I will add it.>`

## Harness

These tune the bbh harness. All are optional; the defaults are shown.

- **Rate limit:** `2` (requests per second per host; the sender never exceeds it, bursts included)
- **OOB callback:** `none` (set `local 7731` for the built-in loopback listener, `listener <base-url> <poll-url>` for your own, or `interactsh <server>`. Without one, SSRF and XXE cells can only close `blocked: no_oob_listener`.)
- **Burp proxy:** `127.0.0.1:8080` (set `none` to send direct; everything still lands in Burp when proxied)
- **Burp MCP:** `http://127.0.0.1:9876` (the SSE endpoint ingest reads history from; `none` to disable Burp ingest)
- **Max concurrent testers:** `2` (class-tester subagents at once; hard cap 10; they share one rate limiter, so more testers never means more load on the target — keep this low and leave a slot free so a verifier can run when a candidate appears)
- **Support hosts:** `none` (extra hosts the agent may navigate to but not send test traffic to, beyond the auto-derived mailboxes/IdP/ngrok/loopback, e.g. a separate SSO domain)

## Environment

Assume Chrome, Firefox, Burp and Playwright are all available and both browsers are logged in as stated above.

If one of them is not reachable, say so in one line, carry on with whatever does work, and re-probe when a task actually needs it. Do not stall the session on it and do not conclude the capability is missing. I fix these in the background and they come back mid-session.

